# Secrets. Never tracked, in any form. `.env.*` rather than a list of suffixes: the list
# held `.env.local` and `.env.*.local` and missed `.env.production`, `.env.prod` and
# `.env.staging`, so `cp .env.example .env.production`, fill in the client secret, then
# `git add -A` put a live credential in the object database and the history. No hook sees a
# human `git add`, and the hard rule covers history as well as the shipped tree, so the
# ignore file is the only control on that path. `.env.example` is the one name that must
# stay trackable: it holds placeholders and the package requires it at its root.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
secrets.env
secrets/

# The same family of names the packaging sweep already refuses inside the staged package,
# so the two controls agree about what a credential looks like. They did not: the sweep
# treats `*.key`, `*.pem`, `id_rsa*`, `*.p12` and `*.pfx` as credential-shaped, while an
# `audit.key` at the repository root was trackable and outside the package allowlist, so
# nothing downstream would ever have seen it. `docs/DEPLOYMENT.md` tells the operator to
# generate the audit key with `openssl rand -hex 32`, which makes redirecting that into a
# file and running `git add -A` the ordinary accident rather than an exotic one. History
# is one-way, so this file is the only control on that path.
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
id_rsa*
# `ssh-keygen -t ed25519` is the current default and writes an extensionless file, so
# neither `*.pem` nor `*.key` matches it. `id_rsa` alone read as covered and was not.
id_ed25519*
id_ecdsa*
id_dsa*
.netrc
.pypirc
secrets.yaml
secrets.yml
credentials.json
client_secret*.json

# Local runtime and build output
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
data/
dist/

# Test and analysis output
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
coverage.xml
sbom.cdx.json
.coverage
htmlcov/
